plan EntityDomains
title   Entity domains: coherent records, conservative repair and honest predictions
status  proposed
goal
    Plain operations get a declared shared-data meaning without author-written cache maintenance.
    One Runtime store keeps record fields coherent across domains. Machines own workflow; the
    domain records places, dependencies, predictions and server effects. Repair is conservative,
    bounded and explainable, and never predicts list membership. Operations are
    @plan.FlowOperations.O1, hydration @plan.ServerRendering.O5 and Stories @plan.Testing.O1.
    Full notes: references/full/EntityDomains.full.plan.txt.
outcomes
    O1  Domains, records, repair and predictions
        An operation describes a request; a domain describes what it means for shared data.
        domain(id, entries) lists read(resource), write(transaction) and listen(stream) entries.
        An operation belongs to at most one domain and machines reach it only as
        O.Domain.entry; operation modules never import entities. Domains never send, commit or
        choose a state. A read needs a result Schema. flow check errors on an operation in two
        domains, on two byKey loaders for one entity, and warns on an unregistered operation
        whose result carries an entity's key fields.

        entity({ id, fields, primaryKey, version? }) declares fields and key. One entity object
        per id; a duplicate is a construction error. Places say where records sit in a result:
        one(Entity, at?) holds one record, list(Entity, at, member?) holds records along a
        path; Entity.pick names an explicit shape. byKey(...) is the record loader.
        derivedFrom(Entity, fields?, where?) declares values computed from records the response
        does not hold; omitted fields means any field, an empty list means membership only.
        Conditions use where (eq, oneOf, contains, between, overlaps, and, principal(),
        constants). An absent optional input drops its constraint. Every list or derived input
        must appear in where, orderBy, pagedBy, ignoring or serverMatches, or construction
        fails; serverMatches names inputs whose filtering only the server knows.

        Order: orderBy ends with every entity key. A list is ordered, unordered or
        server-ranked (optionally narrowed by unaffectedBy). Order is the server's claim and
        the client never sorts. Each response is checked on numbers, booleans, dates and keys;
        a conflict raises HiddenOrder naming the records, and that answer is treated as
        server-ranked for the rest of the Runtime. pagedBy(input, next) declares paging;
        entry.more loads the next page and the lane exposes hasMore. Repair re-fetches from
        page one up to the loaded count and publishes the whole chain at once, serving the old
        chain stale until then. Extra list metadata has no repair guarantee (flow check warns);
        model totals as derived reads.

        Declarations are recorded once at construction. A position is one expression of
        property reads and library operators; ===, arithmetic, templates, method calls,
        conditionals, short-circuits and impure calls fail construction or flow check.
        predicts runs again at write start with the real input.

        Changes: predicts returns Entity.patch(key, fields) or Entity.delete. A patch may only
        touch an entity named in a place or alsoChanges target of that entry; version fields
        cannot be patched. alsoChanges declares extra effects: Entity.all, Entity.where
        (answers whose conditions could overlap) or Entity.fields(key, fields). listen(stream)
        .as([Entities], mapping) may change only those entities. Call-site optimistic,
        setData and clear are rejected on domain operations; writes may only invalidate.

        One record per entity key holds received fields, each with its own version and
        position. Base is server-confirmed data; visible is base plus live predictions
        replayed over each new base. With versions the greater version wins and a lower one
        loses; without, a field from a lookup that started before the stored value landed
        loses. Any response fills an absent field. A differing field that cannot be ordered is
        contested: its loader and holders refetch and the contest is recorded; Flow never
        merges. Predictions change visibility only and never start repairs. Two increments
        stay two increments; a later confirmed write of a field retires older predictions of
        it.

        Each base change is matched against active answers on its old or new values; a missing
        condition field matches. Repair rules: a one answer repairs only on delete; an ordered
        list repairs when a record enters or leaves or an in-condition order field changes; an
        unordered list only on enter or leave; a server-ranked list also on any field change
        except unaffectedBy fields; a derived answer on enter or leave or a relevant field
        change. Merely displaying a field never needs repair. alsoChanges, invalidate,
        reconnect and restore repair every covered answer. A response older than the store
        marks its answer stale. A repair response updates copies and marks answers stale but
        never starts another lookup, so repair never cascades; the next machine request
        refreshes them. A request that started before an affecting change is stale on arrival.
        Each answer has one repair in flight and one trailing refetch. A settled write repairs
        for both the before-prediction and the at-settlement base, so an intervening push is
        never lost. Deltas are consolidated per record per turn but causes are never dropped.

        Write endings. Success installs response fields and ends confirmed predictions in one
        publication, so a value older than a settled write is never shown. Predicted fields
        the response omits stay pending until a confirming read (a later accepted read that
        carries the field, chosen loader first) ends them even at an equal value; if no active
        answer carries the field, the prediction ends and the field is dropped so the next
        reader fetches. Failure or defect removes predictions and repairs affected answers,
        because a failed call may have partly applied. Rejection or cancellation before the
        adapter ran removes predictions without repair. Uncertain (cancelled after invocation,
        response lost, or restored mid-flight) forces the loader and every holder to refetch,
        keeps predictions until that repair lands, then shows server state. unknown delivers
        an Attempt; O.X.retry(select) reissues that exact Attempt and a known-settled attempt
        settles without calling the adapter. basedOn gives each prediction's base version for
        If-Match. Persisted actors acknowledge the Attempt write before the adapter runs.

        Predicting writes queue per record, FIFO across actors, under actor concurrency
        (cancel is a flow check error). A multi-record write joins all its queues together and
        runs only when first in every one; predictions show while queued. Cancelling a queued,
        never-invoked write removes it. An uncertain write releases the next only after its
        forced repair lands.

        Creates return the record and repair matching answers; membership appears only when
        they refetch (no temporary ids or optimistic inserts). A predicted move changes fields
        at once but keeps list membership until repair. A predicted delete hides the record
        everywhere; failure restores it. A confirmed delete leaves a tombstone so older lookups
        cannot resurrect it (until gcTime when versioned); flow check warns on unversioned
        listen. A non-list reference to a deleted record reads a typed Deleted value.

        Records are held while an answer, a loader subscription or a prediction needs them.
        Only confirmed data persists; no prediction is persisted, and restored data never
        overrides live truth. Answer reads are resource lanes plus stale; a failed repair
        keeps data as a failed refresh. Record reads are { data, pending, uncertain,
        unconfirmed }. value outcomes see confirmed data only, so a prediction never fires one
        and saved memory never captures one (@plan.FlowOperations.O2).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/ticket.domain.ts.
    O2  Checkpoint reads and evidence
        Entity.get(checkpoint, key, shape) reads exactly that checkpoint's cut: requested
        fields with data and status, missing fields marked missing, undefined if the record is
        absent. Domain.entry.get returns the lane plus stale and ordered pages, or undefined if
        no active answer exists. Entity.queue returns ordered attempts and queue states.
        Reads never touch live state, advance time or start work.

        Every checkpoint and run end carries repairs and skips for considered answers, plus
        contests, HiddenOrder and self-check findings, in the order delta, domain entry
        position, canonical input key. In-process values, sensitive fields included, are
        faithful; exported evidence replaces sensitive values with keyed digests
        (@plan.Inspection.O5). Explaining a domain runs no adapter and uses the same repair
        classifier as the runtime; asking about an unknown domain, read, entity or key exits 2
        and a truncated trace answers insufficient evidence (@plan.FlowCommandLine.O4).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/queue/queue.story.ts.
    O3  Hydration and lazy domains
        Hydrated confirmed fields keep their versions; positions and frontiers stay local to
        each Runtime. Predictions, queues, tombstones and uncertain marks never transfer, and
        sensitive fields are omitted (@plan.ServerRendering.O3). Absent fields are filled. For
        present versioned fields the greater version wins. For unversioned or equal fields the
        client keeps its data and a differing payload contests the field, refetching its
        loader and holders. Existing answer references win; a different reference marks the
        answer stale. An absent answer is inserted unless the entity changed since ready(), in
        which case it is marked stale. Existing predictions stay. Persisted actors beat
        hydrated ones, hydrated data beats restored data, and a strictly newer versioned
        persisted field still wins. Hydration never fires an initial value event.

        A second tab on the same scope runs without persistence and does no entity peer work
        (@plan.FlowPersistence.O7). Joining a lazy domain happens at admission, before any
        turn sees it: ids, entity objects, registering domains and byKey loaders are checked
        against the adopted graph, structure and restored data publish as one cut, and failure
        rolls back everything (@plan.LazyFeatures.O2). A loader in an inactive entry is
        unavailable, so a field with no carrier is dropped; flow check warns.
    O4  propertyTest and audit mode
        propertyTest(domains, { fixture, inputs, seed, runs, steps }) generates ordinary static
        Stories against fresh Runtimes: successful, failed and uncertain writes; reordered,
        duplicated, failed and empty reads; pushes; reconnect, restore, unsubscribe and
        invalidate; hydrate and mid-run domain adoption (@plan.Testing.O6). The fixture
        oracle holds its own server state, not the domain's rules. At every publication it
        checks: copies of a field agree; at quiet, active answers equal the oracle in declared
        order or are stale with causes; no repair-response lookup and every lookup has causes;
        no accepted field regresses; every displayed unconfirmed value is a live prediction
        with a scheduled or failed confirming read; a record leaves an answer only with a
        recorded reason; identical replays give identical traces; every repair, skip and
        hidden record has causes. Results are pass, fail or inconclusive, never inconclusive
        as success. Failures shrink to literal Stories with the seed.

        Audit is an explicit development and test option, off by default and never in
        production. After each settlement, push or observed change it refetches every active
        answer that was not repaired and compares without publishing: HiddenFilter reports a
        membership difference, UndeclaredEffect names the write, read and missing alsoChanges.
    O5  Bounds
        At most 6 repairs run at once (a Runtime option), started in canonical order: delta,
        domain entry order, input key, stable domain id. A paged repair of N loaded pages is N
        sequential requests in one slot. Each record queues at most 64 waiting writes; if any
        record of a multi-record write would overflow, the whole write is rejected before any
        prediction, queue join or adapter work, with AdmissionOverloaded naming the record
        and limit. Recorded constants and persisted data obey the structural limits of
        @plan.Inspection.O2. Production has no cross-turn execution budget.
decisions
    D1  Domains own shared-data policy
        Predictions replace call-site optimistic and setData; clear is forbidden and writes
        allow invalidate only.
    D2  Confirmed data alone feeds value outcomes
        Predictions and layers never fire value, so saved memory is never an optimistic
        baseline.
    D3  Attempts reconcile explicitly
        unknown carries the Attempt, retry reuses it, and Flow never retries uncertain writes
        itself.
    D4  Per-record queue capacity is finite and atomic
        64 waiting writes per record matches the operation serialize queue; admission rejects
        before any partial join.
constraints
    C1  Tier 1 never invents server answers
        No predicted list membership, instant inserts, offline completeness, client-built
        query results, arbitrary predicate where, field merge, automatic uncertain retry or
        minimum-refetch promise. Machines own uncertain workflow.
    C2  Isolation
        One Runtime per principal and request; positions and frontiers never cross Runtimes;
        no prediction is persisted or hydrated; no version ever regresses.
verification
    V1  Authoring and types: unannotated inference of the reference domains; every rejected
        form (partial order, version in condition or patch, unused input, bad paging, undeclared
        patch entity, call-site optimistic) fails; source check rejects conditionals and
        impurity.
    V2  Repair properties: oracle agreement, coherence, no cascade and monotonic truth hold
        under propertyTest, and removing any one repair rule makes a named variant fail.
    V3  Prediction endings: confirming read (equal value, missing record, no carrier), partial
        failure repair, retirement of older predictions, and unversioned tombstone guard each
        have a targeted test.
    V4  Evidence: checkpoint reads, faithful in-process and digested export values, and
        impact tables agree with observed repairs; audit catches an omitted filter and an
        undeclared effect.
    V5  Hydrate, lazy join and bounds: A-to-B hydrate merges amid client writes, lazy adoption
        with rollback, 6 repair slots, N-request paged chains, and the 64/65 queue boundary
        with no partial joins.
questions
    Q1  Are 6 repair slots and the default of 50 property runs of 40 steps right?
        Recommended: measure Dispatch Desk page churn before changing them.
    Q2  Deferred to build time
        Field-narrowed targets; sampled production audit; per-turn counters and exact event
        field lists; fixture and Story plumbing details; index structure for matching; tombstone
        gc accounting; evidence retention and sequence headroom; oracle evidence handoff.
